Now let me make the fix. I'll rewrite the fallback arm of the match to handle the full range of ECMA-48 escape sequences:

```rust
// Two/three-character escape sequences (ECMA-48):
// - ESC + byte in 0x40..=0x7E (F2..F4 e.g. ESC D, ESC M, ESC c)
// - ESC + byte in 0x30..=0x3F (FE e.g. ESC 7 save cursor, ESC 8 restore,
//   ESC = / ESC > keypad)
// - ESC + intermediate byte in 0x20..=0x2F + final byte in 0x30..=0x7E
//   (e.g. ESC ( B charset selection)
Some(&ch) => {
    chars.next(); // consume the byte after ESC
    if (' '..='/').contains(&ch) {
        // 3-char escape: also consume the final byte
        if let Some(&f) = chars.peek() {
            if ('0'..='~').contains(&f) {
                chars.next();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Note the existing arm was `Some(&ch) if ('@'..='~').contains(&ch) => { chars.next(); }`. The new version handles '@'..='~' by consuming one char (the `if` check only applies to ' '..='/' range). This preserves existing behavior for ESC M, ESC D etc. and adds the missing ranges.

One nuance: an intermediate byte 0x20–0x2F followed by a non-final byte — e.g. `ESC ( ✓`? The final byte must be 0x30–0x7E. If it's a multibyte char, peek gives the full char (first byte ≥ 0x80, not in '0'..='~'), so we don't consume it. Good — no boundary issue since we're using chars not bytes.

